WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.680
This is a normal British terraced house.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.130
It was built around the turn of the 20th century

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:09.250
out of bricks, mortar and a lot of manual
labour.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:12.200
Kitchen, living room and a set of stairs in
the middle

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.890
that lead up to two bedrooms and a bathroom.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.349
And although the exact details might be different,

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:21.319
about one in five houses in Britain look roughly
like this,

00:00:21.319 --> 00:00:24.199
all built in a row.

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:28.220
The trouble is, houses like this weren’t
built to save energy

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.689
or to keep the heat in on a cold winter’s
day.

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:34.080
Energy efficiency wasn’t really a thing
a century ago

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.300
and while there are all sorts of plans to
modernise and retrofit houses:

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.560
How do you know which plans will work?

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.760
How do you do a controlled experiment

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.010
when every house is different and the weather
constantly changes?

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.970
It would be great if you could just, you know...

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.370
tell the sun what to do.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.620
We are in the Salford Energy House

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.240
which is the only whole building in a climate
controlled chamber.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.329
The idea really was to take a Victorian house

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:03.170
and look at different ways that we can make
it more energy efficient.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.390
When you test something on its own, like a
boiler,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.460
you sit it in a lab and you test it and it
will be X% efficient.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.780
When you actually put that in a whole house
or a whole system

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.920
it then interacts with all sorts of other
things

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.990
like the fabric, the radiators, the controls,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.520
so understanding how things fit together as
a whole house

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.250
is really, really important.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.139
This isn’t some close approximation of an
old terraced house made with modern techniques.

00:01:29.139 --> 00:01:32.430
This was a genuine end of terrace house from
out in Manchester.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:36.030
It was taken apart and then rebuilt piece
by piece here

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.859
in an environmental test chamber at the University
of Salford.

00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:43.000
We had to get a bricklayer, the bricklayer
was like 70 years old,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.270
because actually the way that the bricks were
laid in Victorian times,

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.499
nobody does it like that anymore.

00:01:48.499 --> 00:01:51.630
He said he'd not laid bricks like that since
he was a trainee.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.780
We’ve got, I would say, hundreds of sensors.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.280
Every single appliance is monitored individually.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.599
What we can do here is also simulate human
activity.

00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:03.499
We have actuators, so robotic arms

00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:08.420
which will replicate the opening and closing
of doors, fridges, windows.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.560
We can turn appliances on and off.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.330
Here we control the weather.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.850
We have a space next door with a big water
tank

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.990
and there are layers of piping that run round

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.310
that will push rain onto the side of the building

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.490
because rain never falls down, it always comes
sideways.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.560
And then we also have a snow machine,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.490
and we also have wind machines,

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:31.640
and we have a lighting system.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:39.070
So that’s just radiant heating lamps connected
to a theatre lighting control system

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.920
so the sun can pass round the building.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.340
All of those things can make a huge difference
to the way a building performs.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.400
The first really big test we did, which was
a whole house retrofit,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.010
we attached wall insulation, we improved the
doors and the windows

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.570
and we improved the floor

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.670
and we reduced the heating energy by more
than 60%.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.210
There’s always a trade off with experiments
like this.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.680
Yes, heating and cooling a large building

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.040
that is inside another building

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.560
is not massively energy efficient in itself,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.380
but compared to other industrial processes,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.590
it’s not that bad.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.900
And if spending a little energy here saves
a lot elsewhere,

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.940
well that’s probably worth it.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.260
Thank you very much to everyone at the Energy
House

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.030
and the University of Salford School of Built
Environment.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.060
You can see their YouTube channel here

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.290
or pull down the description for more about
them and about the house.

